# Animated grid background for the "Approach" section

## Context

On project case-study pages, "The approach" (`.ping-section--approach`, styled in `ProjectLayout.astro`) is currently a plain, borderless block — just padding, no visual treatment. The user saw a background effect on aphera.co's "Build your system, repeat it" feature section: a faint grid of hairline horizontal/vertical lines that sweeps into view as the section scrolls into the viewport, implemented in a `::before` pseudo-layer toggled by a `.grid-active` class.

The user wants the same mechanic here, adapted to this site's design system:
- Grid line color reuses `--section-border-color` (the existing "weak"/hairline border token already used by `.ping-section--problem`'s outline) — not Aphera's green.
- Same `::before` pseudo-layer + scroll-triggered `.grid-active` class architecture.
- Must work in both light/dark themes (the token already flips per theme) and respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (an existing hard convention on this site — see `runFadeUpStagger` in `src/lib/animations.ts`).

No scroll-triggered animation infrastructure exists anywhere in this codebase today (confirmed via full-repo search) — this is a new pattern, built to match the existing `theme-sweep-up`/`theme-sweep-down` keyframe style (`clip-path: inset()` sweep) and the existing `runFadeUpStagger`/`prefersReducedMotion()` conventions in `src/lib/animations.ts`.

## Step 1 — Branch & issue

Per this project's workflow: create a GitHub issue ("Build animated grid background for Approach section" or similar) via `gh issue create`, then branch off `staging` (fetched fresh) with a `feat/` prefix, e.g. `feat/approach-grid-animation`. Create a draft PR alongside the branch.

## Step 2 — `@keyframes gridSequence` (`src/styles/global.css`)

Add next to the existing `theme-sweep-up`/`theme-sweep-down` keyframes, reusing the same `clip-path: inset()` sweep technique already established there (no new animation primitive introduced):

```css
@keyframes gridSequence {
  0% {
    opacity: 0;
    clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
  }
  40% {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  100% {
    opacity: 1;
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
  }
}
```

Sweeps left-to-right; opacity fades in over the first 40% so the leading edge isn't a hard cut.

## Step 3 — Grid layer CSS (`src/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro`, existing `<style>` block)

Modify `.ping-section--approach` to add a containing block, and add the pseudo-element + active-state rules. Keep Approach borderless/full-bleed (no border-radius) — `.ping-section--problem` is deliberately the one bordered "card" section per the existing code comments; adding a border here would blur that distinction. `overflow: hidden` is added purely so the grid never bleeds a partial cell past the section edge.

```css
.article-body :global(.ping-section--approach) {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  padding-top: var(--ping-section-gap);
  padding-bottom: var(--ping-section-gap);
  padding-left: var(--ping-card-padding);
  padding-right: var(--ping-card-padding);
}

.article-body :global(.ping-section--approach::before) {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image:
    repeating-linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 19px,
      var(--section-border-color) 19px,
      transparent 20px,
      transparent 40px
    ),
    repeating-linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 19px,
      var(--section-border-color) 19px,
      transparent 20px,
      transparent 40px
    );
  mask-image:
    linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%, transparent 100%),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%, transparent 100%);
  mask-composite: intersect;
  -webkit-mask-image:
    linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%, transparent 100%),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%, transparent 100%);
  -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
}

.article-body :global(.ping-section--approach.grid-active::before) {
  animation: gridSequence 1.4s ease-out forwards;
}
```

Start with a single flat opacity via `var(--section-border-color)` directly for both gradients (simplest first pass — matches "small incremental changes"). If it reads too faint/heavy once visible in-browser, a follow-up tweak can split horizontal/vertical line opacity via `color-mix()`. Content inside the section (headings/copy) paints above the grid automatically — `::before` is first in paint order, `z-index: 0` on the pseudo-element, no z-index needed on children.

## Step 4 — Scroll-trigger JS (`src/lib/animations.ts`)

Add a new exported function, following this file's existing two-function shape and its `prefersReducedMotion()` guard convention:

- `observeGridReveal(selector = ".ping-section--approach")`.
- Query all matches; empty-set guard returns early (mirrors `runFadeUpStagger`).
- If `prefersReducedMotion()`: skip the observer, add `.grid-active` to every matched element immediately (grid appears in its finished state with no sweep) — same "snap to end state" behavior `runFadeUpStagger` already uses for reduced motion.
- Otherwise: create one `IntersectionObserver` (threshold ~0.2), and for each entry that intersects, add `.grid-active` to `entry.target` and `unobserve` it — fires once per page view, doesn't replay on scroll-back.

Uses IntersectionObserver directly rather than `motion`'s `inView()` helper — the only job is "toggle a class once on enter," which needs no JS-driven value interpolation (the CSS keyframe owns the actual animation), and it keeps this file's two trigger functions consistent in style rather than introducing a second paradigm.

## Step 5 — Wire it up (`src/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro`)

Add a new `<script>` tag (net-new to this file — it currently has none), matching `BaseLayout.astro`'s existing call-site pattern for `runFadeUpStagger()`:

```astro
<script>
  import { observeGridReveal } from "@/lib/animations";
  observeGridReveal();
</script>
```

Confirmed no collision with the existing `data-animate` fade-up system — that attribute only lives on the page header and body wrapper `<div>` in this file, never on `.ping-section--approach`, so the two animation systems run independently on different elements.

## Verification (manual, in-browser — no unit-testable logic beyond the reduced-motion branch)

1. `bun run dev`, open `/projects/ping` (or whichever slug).
2. Confirm the grid is invisible on load if Approach starts below the fold.
3. Scroll it into view — grid should sweep in once and settle; scrolling away and back should NOT replay it.
4. Repeat on the other two project pages using `.ping-section--approach` (Undercurrent, Watchtower) — same shared layout, should behave identically.
5. Toggle light/dark theme — grid color should follow `--section-border-color`'s per-theme value.
6. DevTools → emulate `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, hard reload — grid should appear immediately, fully visible, no sweep.
7. Visually compare grid line weight against `.ping-section--problem`'s existing hairline border in both themes — should read as the same subtle stroke, not heavier/lighter; tune opacity if not.
8. Confirm `.ping-section--problem` and the solution section are visually unaffected.
9. Resize across breakpoints to confirm `overflow: hidden` clipping and mask edges hold up on narrow widths.
10. Lint before committing (per user's standing preference).

## Files touched

- `src/styles/global.css` — new `@keyframes gridSequence`
- `src/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro` — grid CSS on `.ping-section--approach`, new `<script>` tag
- `src/lib/animations.ts` — new `observeGridReveal()` export

No MDX content changes needed — `.ping-section--approach` already exists on all three project files.

